 \paragraph{Two registered tests.} Both tests used staged registration: a protocol locked and timestamped before the evaluated outcomes existed, and changes recorded in signed, timestamped amendments. The first test (October \const{2026}) locked its claim before the rule was implemented; development results then informed an amended claim and a successor rule, and both claims are reported. The second test (protocol v2) was locked with a Bitcoin timestamp before six fresh market instances and their seeds were drawn from a later block; it adds a matched comparator, a factorial decomposition, stronger attackers, and a replication of the first test's comparison.
 
-\paragraph{Contributions.} Three findings lead. (i) Persistent age-ranked targeting is not materially better than verified-history targeting at the same budget: the development-selected history comparator is pooled-equivalent to R3 within the registered margin. (ii) In the measured factorial contrasts, R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the reduction in the attacker's gain. (iii) Episode-level mixtures of existing public-family attacks expose a welfare weakness that the registered profit-constrained search does not cover. Supporting these are a conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless, measured failure boundaries (stronger and independently authored attackers, coalitions, the terminal-reserve collapse and a tested repair), and two registered tests whose original failure, amendments, and fresh-market replication are documented in a record to be released with the paper.
+\paragraph{Contributions.} Three findings lead. (i) On the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches classified R3 and the development-selected verified-history comparator as pooled-equivalent in attacker gain within $\pm0.05$ under both Q1 attack families. (ii) In the measured factorial contrasts, R3's priority phase and its newest-first targeting substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the reduction in the attacker's gain. (iii) At the headline market, an exploratory analysis of episode-level mixtures of public-family policies found substantially lower empirical gross efficiency than the registered W1 search, at non-negative estimated gain; population profitability remains unestablished. Supporting these are a conditional analysis of when free replacement makes a ban costless, measured failure boundaries (stronger and independently authored attackers, coalitions, the terminal-reserve collapse and a tested repair), and two registered tests whose original failure, amendments, and fresh-market replication are documented in a record to be released with the paper.
 % BEGIN-EV2
 \paragraph{Results in brief.} Q1: pooled over the six fresh markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{int_q1_cat}'' within the registered margin under X7-SC, and likewise under D2+H3: R3 is not materially better than the development-selected verified-history comparator at the same budget. Q2: in the pooled factorial contrasts, the priority phase and newest-first targeting each lower the measured gain (main effects \site{int_q2_p} and \site{int_q2_g}), and either one alone accounts for most of the reduction. Q3: the training-selected attack envelopes have lower measured gains against R3 than against B3 on every evaluated market, a finite-search comparison that does not establish robustness to the searched families; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{int_q3_w0}, and exploratory lotteries over existing attacks, selected on the evaluation records, reach efficiency well below the \site{int_w1} of the registered W1 search at non-negative estimated gain, with public-family attacks alone; the W1 search therefore supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies, and the mixtures' population profitability is not established. Q4: the B3--R3 comparison passes on \site{int_q4_k} of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags; on the other three, R3's estimated advantage is positive but below the registered threshold.
